title: 'ec-utils: handle twisted Edwards z=0 results via IntoAffineSafe instead of panicking'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    On incomplete twisted Edwards curves such as Bandersnatch, HWCD arithmetic
    fed non-subgroup inputs can produce projective points with `z = 0`. These
    have no affine representative, and arkworks' standard
    `From<Projective> for Affine` panics on `z.inverse().unwrap()` for the
    F-exception shapes that miss the `is_zero()` short-circuit.

    The `mul_te` and `msm_te` helpers in `sp-crypto-ec-utils` previously
    called `.into_affine()` unconditionally and would crash on such inputs.
    They now go through a new `IntoAffineSafe` trait that returns `None` on
    `z = 0`, and on the degenerate branch both write the same unified
    fallback: the affine point `(0, -1)`. This is on every TE curve
    (`a·0 + 1 = 1 + 0`), has order 2, and is therefore never in any
    prime-order subgroup, so a downstream `is_in_correct_subgroup_*` check
    on the result catches the degenerate case rather than silently
    accepting an identity-like value.

    The wire format on the FFI boundary is unchanged: still
    `ArkScale<TEAffine>`, no sentinel bit, no new codec. Honest callers
    that subgroup-validate inputs upstream never reach the fallback branch.

    The Bandersnatch hostcall hook (`mul_projective_te`) now also short-
    circuits locally when given a `z = 0` projective input, returning the
    same `(0, -1)` fallback (lifted to projective) instead of attempting
    to serialize an unrepresentable point for the host.
crates:
- name: sp-crypto-ec-utils
  bump: minor
